    "reasoning": "All 16 final answers agree with their own captured Math Academy worked solutions. There are two authored intermediate errors: q-84805 writes P(X>2)=P(X=3)+...+P(X=10), but the sum must end at 15; its subsequent complement calculation and final answer are correct. In q-134529, the displayed expansion of (1+x)^6 ends with x^n instead of x^6; the approximation and percentage error are correct. Independently: q-94154 gives 1-0.9826=0.0174; q-131521 gives 0.1611-0.0233=0.1378; q-94194 gives 0.3783-0.1151=0.2632. Using P(X=k)=\\binom{n}{k}p^k(1-p)^{n-k}, q-96728 gives 0.0256, q-97464 gives 0.27648→0.2765, q-97519 gives 0.4960116→0.4960, and q-97516 gives 0.098816→0.0988. For q-97521, 1-(0.5)^7=0.9921875→0.9922. For q-134529, the three-term approximation is 1-0.6+0.15=0.55; 100|0.55-0.531441|/0.531441 rounds to 3.49%. For q-134649, 1024+1280(-0.11)+640(-0.11)^2=890.944. For q-160776, substitution gives components 2e^0=2, sqrt(4)=2, and (-2)^4=16. For q-162704, the composed transformation is diag(-3,1), so a+b=-2. For q-89366, success means red or green, with probability 0.6; binom(12,5)(0.6)^5(0.4)^7 rounds to 0.101. For q-130744, summing the binomial probabilities for 6, 7, and 8 successes rounds to 0.0006. For q-84805, 1-[(0.8)^15+15(0.2)(0.8)^14+105(0.2)^2(0.8)^13] rounds to 0.602. For q-84804, 1-[(0.65)^14+14(0.35)(0.65)^13] rounds to 0.979. The source-error judgment concerns intermediate writing, with no disagreement on any requested final value.",
